是啊。

00:51:06.760 --> 00:51:07.760
Yeah.
是啊。

00:51:07.760 --> 00:51:08.760
I don't think you've ever said that.
我觉得你从没说过这个。

00:51:08.760 --> 00:51:09.760
I don't care.
我不在乎。

00:51:09.760 --> 00:51:10.760
I need to say that anymore.
我不需要再说了。

00:51:10.760 --> 00:51:11.760
And if you did, it's a wonderful imperfection.
如果你说过了，这也是一种美妙的不完美。

00:51:11.760 --> 00:51:12.760
Right.
没错。

00:51:12.760 --> 00:51:13.760
You're repeating it.
你在重复它。

00:51:13.760 --> 00:51:14.760
Yes, exactly.
对，没错。

00:51:14.760 --> 00:51:20.079
But no, we, as humans, we want to, we want, we think perfection is like keep that imperfection out.
但不是的，作为人类，我们想要，我们想，我们认为完美就是把那些不完美排除在外。

00:51:20.079 --> 00:51:24.760
But in the grand sense of things, imperfections included, he has a book called Everything Belongs.
从宏观角度来看，包括不完美在内，他有一本书叫《万物皆有归属》。

00:51:24.760 --> 00:51:25.760
Right?
对吧？

00:51:25.760 --> 00:51:26.760
Yeah.
对。

00:51:26.760 --> 00:51:33.760
So I think in, in, when you look at the, I think that's what a lot of people are saying when when they write in and leave us voice notes about how I meant it.
所以我觉得，当你看到……我觉得很多人写信给我们，或者留下语音留言说他们对《老爸老妈浪漫史》的看法时，表达的正是这个意思。

00:51:33.760 --> 00:51:38.039
And mother is like, it kept them going because you see the imperfection in the struggle.
母亲就像是……它支撑着他们走下去，因为你在挣扎中看到了不完美。

00:51:38.039 --> 00:51:38.880
Why is this happening?
为什么会这样？

00:51:38.880 --> 00:51:40.239
This shouldn't be happening this way.
事情不该是这样的。

00:51:40.239 --> 00:51:42.480
But you see that it was all leading somewhere.
但你发现这一切其实都是命中注定的指引。

00:51:42.480 --> 00:51:44.519
It's mostly about imperfection.
这主要关于不完美。

00:51:44.519 --> 00:51:46.159
Yeah, it's mostly about imperfection.
对，主要关于不完美。

00:51:46.159 --> 00:51:58.159
But it also says that that imperfection not contrary to the idea that it means the universe is chaotic and disordered.
但它也说明，这种不完美并不意味着宇宙是混乱和无序的。

00:51:58.159 --> 00:52:09.420
It actually says, no, in spite, no, because of that, and because of the long tail of looking at things with some perspective, you see the universe is benevolent and ordered.
它实际上在说，不，正相反，恰恰相反，而且从长远和某种宏观视角来看，你会发现宇宙其实是充满善意且有序的。

00:52:09.420 --> 00:52:10.960
Yes, I totally agree.
是的，我完全同意。

00:52:10.960 --> 00:52:26.840
And I think literally we manifested it, whether we meant to or not, when Ted shows up in this completely imperfect outfit with bleach stains, right, they meet in tuxedos and they meet and he's a knight in shining armor, and she's the princess in the tower.
而且我觉得，无论我们是否有意，我们确实让它成真了：当泰德穿着这身完全不完美、带着漂白剂渍的衣服出现时，对吧，他们在一群穿燕尾服的人中间相遇，他就像穿着闪亮盔甲的骑士，而她是高塔里的公主。

00:52:26.840 --> 00:52:33.760
And he goes in and she's covered in flour, putting frosting on cupcakes and he's wearing a shirt with stands on it.
他走进去时，她身上沾满了面粉，正在给纸杯蛋糕抹糖霜，而他穿着一件有污渍的衬衫。

00:52:33.760 --> 00:52:36.599
And that's why I think that moment gets me so much when she says, oh, thank God.
这就是为什么我觉得那一刻深深打动了我，当她说，哦，谢天谢地。

00:52:36.599 --> 00:52:39.480
Cause she's kind of like, here's the real me.
因为她有点像是，这才是真实的我。

00:52:39.480 --> 00:52:43.159
I'm willing to roll the dice on this being imperfect.
我愿意为这种不完美去冒险。

00:52:43.159 --> 00:52:44.840
Look at, just look at me.
看，看着我。

00:52:44.840 --> 00:52:47.159
And she's not in a perfect moment either.
而且她当时也不是处于一个完美的时刻。

00:52:47.159 --> 00:52:51.840
And they realize they're going to like, they're going to exist in imperfection together.
然后他们意识到，他们将要……他们将要在不完美中一起生活。

00:52:51.840 --> 00:52:57.199
They're gonna come, they're gonna risk stepping out of that one little night they were trying to put in like a little hot house.
他们要来，他们要冒险走出那个他们试图小心翼翼呵护在温室里的小小的夜晚。

00:52:57.199 --> 00:53:08.250
And even this is like garment as metaphor for the whole episode, like a bleach stain sweatshirt is that thing in an object, right?
甚至连这件衣服都成了整集剧的隐喻，比如一件有漂白剂渍的卫衣，它就是这样一个具体的物件，对吧？

00:53:08.250 --> 00:53:12.929
It is like you are more attached to that sweatshirt once it has the bleach stain on it.
一旦那件卫衣有了漂白剂渍，你反而会对它更有感情。

00:53:12.929 --> 00:53:17.449
It becomes somehow more perfect with its flaw, right?
它带着瑕疵，却反而变得更加完美了，对吧？

00:53:17.449 --> 00:53:26.369
And it becomes more, it's like, it's your, it's a sign of your own humanity in your own imperfection.
而且它变得更……这就像是你自身的人性以及不完美的象征。

00:53:26.369 --> 00:53:37.090
I also, it mirrors, it kind of rhymes with him in the pilot going with all of them to go get Robin.
我也觉得，这跟试播集中他和所有人一起去接罗宾的场景有点呼应，异曲同工。

00:53:37.090 --> 00:53:38.730
They're all in the taxi.
他们都在出租车里。

00:53:38.730 --> 00:53:42.650
Rebecca was like, they always go everywhere and with Ted and in a taxi together.
莉莉当时就说，他们总是和泰德一起坐出租车去任何地方。

00:53:42.650 --> 00:53:45.409
They're wildly co-dependent and enmeshed.
他们有着疯狂的共生关系，紧密纠缠在一起。

00:53:45.409 --> 00:53:46.929
It's lovely though, it's so funny.
不过这很可爱，太搞笑了。

00:53:46.929 --> 00:53:48.730
No, it's adorable.
不，这太讨人喜欢了。

00:53:48.730 --> 00:54:00.539
But there's something, you know, And then his, I don't know, like that speech is so lovely, but I mean Barney's, you know, crapper get off the pot is such a good treacle cutter.
但有某种……你知道的，然后他的……我不知道，那段演讲太美好了，不过我是说巴尼的“要么干脆点，要么拉倒”真是个很好的清醒剂。

00:54:00.539 --> 00:54:03.500
That is the definition of a good treacle cutter.
那绝对是一个优质清醒剂的定义。

00:54:03.500 --> 00:54:05.139
That's it, that's it, right there.
就是这样，就是这样，当场见效。

00:54:05.139 --> 00:54:06.219
Yeah.
对。

00:54:06.219 --> 00:54:17.420
But I remember, and maybe this is like Ashley just made me as an actor feel so good, but like, there's something about watching me all these years later.
但我记得——也许这只是阿什莉让我作为演员感觉太棒了——多年后回看这一集，还是有些特别的感觉。

00:54:17.579 --> 00:54:23.840
I remember seeing this episode and I really liked just how I went and got her.
我记得看这一集时，我真的很喜欢我是怎么去把她找回来的。

00:54:23.840 --> 00:54:30.000
You know, there's something so satisfying about his masculine energy that is just forward.
你知道的，他那种勇往直前的男性魅力有一种极其强烈的满足感。

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:30.840
It's like he's in hair.
就像他在弄发型。

00:54:30.840 --> 00:54:31.760
He's like Han Solo in this one.
他在这集里就像韩·索罗。

00:54:31.760 --> 00:54:33.360
He's Luke Skywalker most of the time.
他平时大部分时间都是卢克·天行者。

00:54:33.360 --> 00:54:35.500
He's Han Solo in this one.
但他在这集里是韩·索罗。

00:54:35.500 --> 00:54:40.610
Yeah, and there's something great about when Ted, like, crabs are getting off the pot.
对，当泰德“要么干脆点，要么拉倒”的时候，有一种很棒的感觉。

00:54:40.610 --> 00:54:50.730
Like, when he's in decision, when he's out of his overthinky indecisive, when he's actually in action, He's an incredibly appealing guy.
就像当他不再优柔寡断、不再过度思考，而是真正付诸行动时，他是一个极具吸引力的家伙。

00:54:50.730 --> 00:54:56.889
And you can understand why he would know 83% of the time what to say to a woman.
你能理解为什么他有83%的把握知道该对女人说些什么。

00:54:56.889 --> 00:54:58.610
Yeah, yeah, totally.
对，对，完全如此。

00:54:58.610 --> 00:55:04.369
And I think that it's one of the most comfortable I've seen you in an episode.
而且我觉得这是我在一集里看到你最自然的状态之一。

00:55:04.369 --> 00:55:05.210
Do you know what I mean?
你知道我的意思吗？

00:55:05.210 --> 00:55:07.170
You just seemed very comfortable in this one.
你在这集里显得非常松弛。

00:55:07.170 --> 00:55:13.769
Like I don't know, I think you and Ashley just, there was an energy that had unlocked that was like a little different in you in this one.
我不知道，我觉得你和阿什莉之间，有一种被彻底激活的能量，让你在这集里展现出了一点点不同。

00:55:13.769 --> 00:55:16.010
And it's just, it's so lovely.
真的非常美好。

00:55:16.010 --> 00:55:23.849
And Ted, if you think about it, it's the embodiment of what you always say about Ted, which is like the resilience is sort of the spirit animal of Ted.
如果你仔细想想，泰德就是你经常提到的那种特质的化身，那就是“韧性”可以说是泰德的精神图腾。

00:55:23.849 --> 00:55:31.170
He just got so fucked up in the previous episode by how hard he tried to get Robin to me his date to the sweating.
他在上一集里因为拼命想让罗宾做他的舞伴而弄得大汗淋漓、狼狈不堪。

00:55:31.170 --> 00:55:42.210
And kind of in the blink of an eye, he is able to scrape himself up from that defeat and be so present and kind of swagger into this new challenge and this new chapter.
而转眼之间，他就能从那次失败中重新振作起来，如此专注地活在当下，带着自信的大摇大摆迎接这个新的挑战和新的篇章。

00:55:42.210 --> 00:55:44.570
And I think you played it so great.
我觉得你演得太棒了。

00:55:44.570 --> 00:55:49.250
I just think you owned the sort of like the relief of moving on to something new for Ted.
我真的觉得你完美诠释了泰德迈向新生活的释然。

00:55:49.250 --> 00:55:57.210
Yeah, but it's funny, you know, Barney's vision for their night was like, we're both gonna like get these bridesmaids and Ted's like, no, I'm actually gonna get a new girlfriend.
对，但很有趣，你知道的，巴尼对他们那个夜晚的设想是，我们俩都要去泡到这些伴娘，而泰德却说，不，其实我要去找个新女朋友。

00:55:57.210 --> 00:55:58.050
Yeah.
对。

00:55:58.050 --> 00:56:01.650
I'm gonna have a very serious relationship that's gonna start tonight.
我要开始一段非常认真的关系，今晚就开始。

00:56:01.650 --> 00:56:02.730
Starts right now.
现在就开始。

00:56:07.960 --> 00:56:10.440
This is how we like to end these episodes.
这就是我们喜欢结束这些单集的方式。

00:56:10.440 --> 00:56:23.247
We get so many wonderful letters filled with Um, just so much love and gratitude and appreciation and insight and, uh, we're moved every single week by these, uh, keep in common.
我们收到了那么多充满爱意、感激、欣赏和深刻见解的精彩来信，呃，每一周我们都被这些信件深深触动，请大家继续发信来。

00:56:23.247 --> 00:56:35.840
Uh, you can go to how we made your mother.com and go to contact and you can either leave us, uh, a voice note or, uh, write something and hopefully it'll make it onto the air.
呃，你可以访问 how we made your mother.com，点击“联系我们”，你可以给我们留一条语音留言，或者写点什么，希望它能被节目播出。

00:56:35.840 --> 00:56:40.440
We really, this is a really fun part of this for us is to hear from you guys.
听到你们的声音对我们来说真的是一件非常有趣的事。

00:56:40.440 --> 00:56:41.719
So it's a gift.
所以这是一个礼物。

00:56:41.719 --> 00:56:42.119
Thank you.
谢谢你们。

00:56:42.119 --> 00:56:42.719
Keep a comment.
请继续发信。

00:56:42.719 --> 00:56:44.719
That's only dear him.
那是《老爸老妈浪漫史》的粉丝。

00:56:44.960 --> 00:56:46.239
This is the story of how I met you.
这是我如何遇见你的故事。

00:56:46.599 --> 00:56:47.679
It's 2011.
那是2011年。

00:56:48.079 --> 00:56:55.440
I'm 18 fresh off the train from a small town in India, wide eyed and overwhelmed in Mumbai.
我18岁，刚从印度一个小镇坐火车出来，在孟买睁大双眼，感到不知所措。

00:56:55.920 --> 00:56:58.960
For context, it's like moving from a small town to New York.
打个比方，这就像从一个小镇搬到纽约。

00:56:59.320 --> 00:57:01.239
Everything was new, fast and exciting.
一切都是崭新的、快节奏的，令人兴奋。

00:57:01.679 --> 00:57:06.519
Soon enough, a girl I was very much interested in introduced me to how I met your mother.
很快，一个我很感兴趣的女孩把我带入了《老爸老妈浪漫史》的世界。

00:57:06.920 --> 00:57:08.119
It was a funny accident.
这是一个搞笑的意外。

00:57:08.119 --> 00:57:15.000
We were at lunch when she took a sip of piping hot soup, winced and yelled, ouchy in my mouth.
我们在吃午饭时，她喝了一口滚烫的汤，龇牙咧嘴地大喊：“好烫，烫到我嘴了！”

00:57:15.000 --> 00:57:17.559
And burst out laughing.
然后放声大笑。

00:57:17.559 --> 00:57:19.360
I had no idea what she was talking about.
我当时完全不知道她在说什么。

00:57:19.360 --> 00:57:25.880
After an enthusiastic 15 minute sales pitch from her and a quick 10 minute hard drive transfer, I hit play on the pilot.
在她热情洋溢地推销了15分钟并快速传输了10分钟的硬盘后，我按下了试播集的播放键。

00:57:25.880 --> 00:57:36.760
Little did I know that this 720 P file from a world I had no context for would change my life or as Barney would put it, it taught me how to live.
我当时万万没有想到，这个来自一个我毫无概念的世界的720P视频文件会改变我的人生，或者用巴尼的话说，它教会了我如何生活。

00:57:36.760 --> 00:57:38.619
At the time, my English wasn't great.
当时我的英语并不是很好。

00:57:38.619 --> 00:57:41.019
I could barely string two proper sentences together.
我甚至很难完整地说出两个像样的句子。

00:57:41.019 --> 00:57:47.420
So the first thing I picked up from him, yum, was the language, but what kept me hooked was the world Carter, Craig, and Pam had built.
所以我从他身上学到的第一件事，就是“yum”（真香/好吃），这是语言，但真正让我着迷的是卡特、克雷格和帕姆构建的那个世界。

00:57:47.420 --> 00:57:54.900
The way Ted saw his life as a story in the making, that shaped me, his nostalgia, his deep reflections, and future Ted's narration.
泰德将自己的人生视作一个正在进行中的故事的那种视角塑造了我，他的怀旧、他的深沉反思，以及未来泰德的旁白。

00:57:54.900 --> 00:57:56.380
It all stayed with me.
这一切都留在了我心里。

00:57:56.380 --> 00:58:03.500
Now, when I'm with my friends or caught in an interesting moment, I remind myself that a couple years down the line, this will be a wonderful memory.
现在，每当我跟朋友在一起，或者陷入某个有趣的时刻时，我都会提醒自己，几年之后，这将是一段美好的回忆。

00:58:03.500 --> 00:58:06.900
And when it comes back unannounced, it'll make me smile.
而当它不期而至地浮现时，会让我会心一笑。

00:58:06.900 --> 00:58:09.179
Ted's words have broken my heart, made me laugh.
泰德的话曾让我心碎，也让我大笑。

00:58:09.179 --> 00:58:13.480
And now, after seeing just a little more of life, they feel more real than ever.
而现在，在经历更多的人生之后，这些话感觉比以往任何时候都更加真实。

00:58:13.480 --> 00:58:16.699
Sometimes it's like he's talking about me.
有时候感觉他就像是在谈论我。

00:58:16.699 --> 00:58:19.739
And then of course, there's the writing, the brilliance of it all.
当然，还有剧本创作，那种无与伦比的才华。

00:58:19.739 --> 00:58:29.820
The things himium introduced, the playbook, the bro code, the drum roll, future Ted, future Marshall, you gave me a whole new vocabulary that was funny, fresh, and profound at the same time.
《老爸老妈浪漫史》引入的那些概念：把妹手册、兄弟准则、鼓点、未来泰德、未来马歇尔，你们赋予了我一整套既有趣、新鲜又深奥的新词汇。

00:58:29.820 --> 00:58:35.420
I may or may not have stolen life lessons from the show and pass them off as my own wisdom to friends in need.
我可能曾从这部剧里偷师过一些生活经验，并把它们当作自己的大道理传授给需要帮助的朋友。

00:58:35.420 --> 00:58:37.420
Let's call it borrowing.
我们姑且称之为“借用”吧。

00:58:37.420 --> 00:58:43.340
Now at 29 as a filmmaker, I find myself going back to the show, not just for comfort, but for lessons.
如今29岁的我成了一名电影制作人，我发现自己会不断重温这部剧，不仅是为了寻求慰藉，更是为了学习。

00:58:43.340 --> 00:58:49.460
The way you took eccentric, lovable characters and pushed them into raw, vulnerable moments, it's magic.
你们将那些古怪可爱的人物推入真实而脆弱的时刻，这种处理方式简直是魔法。

00:58:49.460 --> 00:59:01.699
And somehow, even after a dozen rewatches and knowing every episode inside out, I still discover new details, a subtle reaction, a cool edit call, a background character doing something hilarious.
不知为何，即使在刷了十多遍、对每一集都了如指掌之后，我依然能发现新的细节：一个微妙的反应、一个精彩的剪辑点、背景角色做出的某个搞笑动作。

00:59:01.699 --> 00:59:05.579
It's like a little, whoa, that was a cool moment that I enjoyed by myself.
这就像是一个小小的惊喜：“哇，刚才那个瞬间真棒，只有我一个人发现了。”

00:59:05.579 --> 00:59:10.340
Also watching the bloopers and 20 things you missed in hymnium videos is still one of my favorite things to do.
此外，看花絮以及“你错过了《老爸老妈浪漫史》的20个细节”之类的视频，依然是我最喜欢做的事情之一。

00:59:10.340 --> 00:59:16.940
So to everyone who worked on the show, thank you for the laughs, the heartbreaks, the ridiculous inside jokes, and the life lessons I never saw coming.
所以对于所有为这部剧付出过努力的人，谢谢你们带给我的欢笑、心碎、那些荒谬的内部笑话，以及我始料未及的人生教训。

00:59:16.940 --> 00:59:27.170
You had no idea you were shaping a kid in India, one episode at a time, but you did, with all my heart, Hardik, Sajwani.
你们当时绝不会想到，你们正潜移默化地塑造着印度的一个孩子，一集接一集，但你们确实做到了，发自内心地，Hardik，Sajwani。

00:59:27.170 --> 00:59:29.409
Oh my gosh, beautiful, wow.
噢天哪，太美妙了，哇。

00:59:29.409 --> 00:59:29.961
Beautiful.
太美妙了。

00:59:29.961 --> 00:59:31.010
Thank you for that.
谢谢你读这段留言。

00:59:31.010 --> 00:59:32.269
Thank you for sharing that story.
谢谢你分享这个故事。

00:59:32.269 --> 00:59:34.130
And boy, is your English good now.
而且天哪，你现在的英语说得真好。

00:59:34.130 --> 00:59:36.230
See what good teachers you were.
看看你们是多么优秀的老师。

00:59:36.230 --> 00:59:37.070
That's incredibly well written.
这写得真是难以置信地好。

00:59:37.070 --> 00:59:37.909
That's so good.
太棒了。

00:59:37.909 --> 00:59:38.909
Yeah.
是啊。

00:59:38.909 --> 00:59:40.070
Wow.
哇。

00:59:40.070 --> 00:59:42.230
Yeah, I mean, two things that came up.
对，我是说，有两件事浮现在我脑海。

00:59:42.230 --> 00:59:52.969
One is, you know, I'd mentioned this before, but like the world of stage 22 on the Fox lot, all those years was so insulated.
一个是，你知道的，我之前提到过，福克斯片场22号摄影棚的世界，这些年来一直是那么与世隔绝。

00:59:52.969 --> 00:59:55.809
And it felt, you know, it's a huge stage, but it felt very small.
而且你会觉得，你知道的，那是个很大的摄影棚，但感觉非常小。

00:59:55.809 --> 01:00:09.269
It felt like this little village, this little bustling village with all sorts of, you know, craftspeople and crew members and writers and actors and just everyone milling about making this show.
感觉就像一个小村庄，一个小而热闹的村庄，里面有各种各样的人，你知道的，工匠、剧组人员、编剧、演员，还有所有人都在四处走动，制作这部剧。

01:00:09.269 --> 01:00:13.190
And it felt so small, you forget, it was being beamed all over the world.
而且它感觉那么小，以至于你都会忘记，它正在向全世界播送。

01:00:13.190 --> 01:00:14.030
Yeah.
对。

01:00:14.030 --> 01:00:14.869
All over the world.
全世界。

01:00:14.869 --> 01:00:17.789
2011, it was still on, you know, when he got ahold of it.
2011年，当他接触到这部剧的时候，它还在热播。

01:00:17.789 --> 01:00:20.630
But it was affecting people's lexicons.
但它正在影响人们的词汇量。

01:00:20.630 --> 01:00:23.389
It was affecting people, changing people's sense of humor.
它在影响人们，改变人们的幽默感。

01:00:23.389 --> 01:00:33.289
And I've also heard from so many people, I mean, so many people who say, how I met your mother, that's how I learned English.
而且我也听很多人说过，我是说有太多人说，《老爸老妈浪漫史》，那就是我学英语的方式。

01:00:33.289 --> 01:00:37.670
I've heard it over and over again, and it floors me equally every time.
我听过无数次了，每次听到我都同样感到震惊。

01:00:37.670 --> 01:00:41.769
It hasn't, the amazement has not diminished every time in here that it's, what an honor.
这种惊叹并没有减少，每次听到时都觉得，这真是无上的荣誉。

01:00:41.769 --> 01:00:46.849
And it's like, we weren't writing this, thinking people would be learning English from it.
这就好像，我们当初写剧本时，可没想过人们会通过它来学英语。

01:00:46.849 --> 01:00:52.889
It's just like, it's so not a consequence we imagined would come of this, because it just can't, too many ripples.
这根本不是我们想象中会带来的结果，因为这不可能预料到，产生的涟漪实在太多了。

01:00:52.889 --> 01:01:09.170
It's probably, they say that if you can, the true test of whether you really understand a different language, if you can watch a comedy in that language and laugh and get the jokes, because so much of humor is misdirection or irony.
可能就像他们说的，如果你能——检验你是否真正理解一门不同语言的真正标准，就是看你能不能用那门语言看喜剧并笑出来、听懂笑话，因为幽默中包含着大量的误导或反讽。

01:01:09.170 --> 01:01:15.969
Like, it's not, if you just have a rudimentary understanding of the language, you're not gonna get a lot of humor that's popular in the culture.
就像，如果你对这门语言只有粗浅的理解，你是无法理解很多在该文化中流行的幽默的。

01:01:15.969 --> 01:01:29.889
So if you can really understand it comedically, that's why I think it is a really good teacher, because it's like, you know, I would, this would be something for AI, but like, I bet how I met your mother had a larger vocabulary than a lot of other shows all at the time, like sitcoms.
所以，如果你真的能在喜剧层面上理解它，这就是为什么我认为它真是一个很好的老师，因为我觉得，你知道的，虽然这可以说是AI能做的事，但我敢说，《老爸老妈浪漫史》比当时许多其他剧集的词汇量都要大，比如情景喜剧。

01:01:29.889 --> 01:01:38.250
Like, you know, not every show's dropping, like making ineffable, like a hilarious plot point, you know, it had a lot of poly-syllabic words.
就像，你知道的，不是每部剧都会抛出像“不可言喻（ineffable）”这样的词，把它变成一个搞笑的剧情点，你知道的，里面有很多多音节词。

01:01:38.250 --> 01:01:40.690
Marshall, you know, using legal terms and stuff.
还有马歇尔，你知道的，总是用法律术语之类的。

01:01:40.690 --> 01:01:50.449
So I think, I really love what he was saying in this whole thing, but I am, I continue to be delighted.
所以我觉得，我真的很喜欢他在整段话里所表达的，但我依然感到非常高兴。

01:01:50.449 --> 01:01:55.170
I have a friend, Nima, who grew up in Tehran until he was 15 and then moved to Canada.
我有个朋友叫尼玛（Nima），他在德黑兰长大到15岁，然后搬到了加拿大。

01:01:55.170 --> 01:02:12.389
And he didn't tell me until, I actually met him the same weekend I met my wife, but he is now a very dear friend and he didn't reveal to me until years into our friendship that he learned English from how I met your mother in Scrubs.
直到——其实我认识他的那个周末正是我遇到我妻子的那个周末，但他现在是我非常亲密的朋友，直到我们认识多年后，他才向我透露，他是通过《老爸老妈浪漫史》和《实习医生风云》来学习英语的。

01:02:12.389 --> 01:02:21.530
And how I met your mother were the two shows that he just knew backwards and forwards and kind of taught him about America and all this stuff.
而《老爸老妈浪漫史》就是他倒背如流的两部剧之一，它在某种程度上教会了他关于美国的一切以及诸如此类的事情。

01:02:21.530 --> 01:02:22.369
So.
所以。

01:02:22.369 --> 01:02:23.210
Oh my God, what an honor.
噢天哪，这真是无上的荣誉。

01:02:23.210 --> 01:02:27.969
I mean, what an honor that it could be part of someone's journey in learning English and that it continues to mean something.
我是说，能够成为某人英语学习旅程的一部分，并且它能继续保持意义，这是何等的荣誉。

01:02:27.969 --> 01:02:29.449
Pardic, thank you so much.
Pardic，非常感谢你。

01:02:29.449 --> 01:02:31.170
That was an amazing letter.
那是一封太棒的信了。

01:02:31.170 --> 01:02:36.039
Thanks for letting us be part of your story, honestly, it means a lot.
谢谢你让我们成为你故事的一部分，说实话，这意义重大。

